vement preached about  what Hinduism was what it took to be a  good Hindu  and for those reasons I was interested  in seeing how did this party go about  the difficult task of appealing to these  voters for whom we could offer little in  terms of its economic policies which  largely favored the rich in terms of its  ideology which largely favored upper  castes so given that situation what  could they in fact do and the basic  argument that I make in the paper which  is taken from my dissertation was that  it wasn't anything that the political  party did but instead work that it's  part that its partners in the wider  social movement of Hindu nationalism did  which was to provide basic social  services so very basic schooling very  basic medical care the type that's  common among other religious movements  worldwide  it was those services that really  created the breakthrough for Hindu  nationalists in parts of India and so  the paper that that you referenced was  looking at how that process actually  took place and how did you come to write  the paper what about this topic  interests you how did you get drawn to  it well I actually didn't think about  writing it when I went to graduate  school but what interested me was that  the 2004 national elections in India  took place early on while I was in  graduate school and the results in that  results it was the Hindu nationalist  party that actually lost the election  and so I was reading headlines about the  elections that were trying to make sense  of why they had lost because most  pundits had expected them to win and the  consensus was there was actually the  poor who had voted them out of office  the party had run on a platform called  India shining which was meant to  showcase India's achievement and arrival  on the world stage and while that was  seen to play well with middle-class  urban voters it was seen to kind of  denigrate the the experiences of the  poor who had been continuing to suffer  even through India's economic boom so  pundits said this was the poor voting  out the rich but when I was looking at  some of the survey data that came out of  that election that didn't seem to be the  case and there were several states in  which Hindu nationalists actually the  lead vote getter among these lower  castes and that just didn't fit with  what experts in conventional wisdom  understood to be their appeal this was  always understood as an urban middle  class upper caste party so how are they  winning these voters and so trying to  explain that that's what got me  interested  just a puzzle a very local puzzle based  on these election results and it turned  into something bigger and more  substantial so let's talk about your  methodology how did you do your research  it was a little bit tricky because of  the the research topic so it took  several steps I mean the first thing  that I was looking at was basically  trying to understand how and where these  service organizations or organizations  are actually strong and so that involved  getting a lot of data looking at you  know where these chapters actually built  in which states in India which districts  in India are they the strongest and are  those also the states and districts in  which the party is doing well with these  voters and so that was the kind of first  national level look based on those  results based on finding out which areas  of the country were these organizations  in and where was the party doing well I  selected some cases for more in-depth  analysis and so basically what I did was  I looked at one case the case the papers  based on where they succeeded where  these service organizations were present  and they clearly seemed to be  corresponding to higher votes for the  Hindu nationalists but I also looked at  a couple of cases where they've tried  these service organizations were there  but there weren't the resulting  electoral gains that we would expect for  the party arm because I wanted to see  where the situations in which it seems  to work and where does it not work and  then within each of these states I did a  mixture of my own surveys to see whether  where the service organizations were  whether the individuals that they were  getting and benefiting were the ones who  were actually turning their votes for  the BJP for the Hindu nationalists and I  also did a lot of interviews with the  providers you know trying to track how  they went about their service provision  how did they actually get people who  didn't trust them lower caste people who  were untrusting of upper caste activists  to actually even come to their schools  even accept the benefits that they were  offering and also talked to ordinary  voters to say you know what do they make  of these services what did they make of  the party based on these services and  things like that there's a lot of  difference okay and who are the  providers and and what are the services  that you're talking about some example  sure so the providers are mostly  activists with the Hindu nationalist  movement and in the first generation  these tend to be upper caste urban men  who often leave their families located  in cities and travel into areas where  lower castes and tribals Indian Indian  tribal populations live which are often  very far away from their home and so  this obviously involves a lot of  sacrifice and is due only to their very  intense ideological commitment to the  movement and so they tend to be young  and often have very long stints of 10 15  20 years sometimes their family will  later come out and join them sometimes  they don't have families their wedded  only to the movement so that's who they  are and I spend a lot of time with these  guys trying to figure out why they did  this with the decided to do this and how  successful what kind of obstacles they  faced was it hard to find these  individuals that's I mean that's pretty  that's an amazing um its thing it's it's  it's hard initially but these networks  are very tight and they're very tightly  controlled to the central movement so if  you can make inroads which I did while I  was doing some initial fieldwork in the  capital a lot of this is through words  of mouth it's it's getting cell phone  numbers or getting addresses and showing  up and waiting and often you get turned  away initially but over time that's  often what took the longest time was  just making that first step but once you  do and you're accepted and they and they  trust you they're a very tightly  networked very organized group and so  you can very quickly meet many more  people within the network the services  that they offer are actually very basic  we're not talking about you know big  school buildings and fancy hospitals  most of the time we're talking about a  single activist located in a village who  will run a school a one teacher school  but there's not even off in a physical  building so he will just take mostly he  sometimes she will just take a space any  open space in the village a room in a  house if somebody is willing to donate  that and teach class and that for three  to four hours a day sometimes longer the  hospice care is likewise the very same  they get they go to a central dispensary  run by the organization once a month get  very basic medical supplies and are  distributing them but this is in the  context of Indian villages where you  know the number one killers are highly  preventable whether it's de century or  or malaria and so even you know giving  out queen een or just mixing simple  sugar solutions for them can have a big  effect so it's very basic services but  they're still quite in demand and the  places that we're talking about  okay so correct me if I'm wrong  basically you have these men going out  into the country where the lower caste  people are living and they're providing  these services and are they trying to  talk to them about politics and you know  here I mean how does that work exactly  go