icult thing to  measure but ended up being a very  important part of the story yeah it's  it's very interesting to me because I  would think that the people you know  I'll call it a village would catch on to  the fact that this purse  is really affiliated with this political  party so that's why we're very  interesting phenomenon no I think so too  and this is something I'm wrestling with  the first thing to note is that this is  a relatively recent strategy so it's  only in the last two decades that these  service chapters have really been built  up and it's really only in the last five  or ten years that we're seeing the  switches to this party so that's one or  two election cycles the question of  whether this can be sustained over time  whether they can retain this neutral  status while constantly lobbying for one  side I think remains to be seen I think  it's one of the great weaknesses of  social services as an electoral strategy  I will say that I don't think it's that  voters are being blindly fooled I think  it's more that they begin to respect  these people and therefore respect their  opinions and think that maybe the party  is worth a shot right now the flip side  of that is if the party keeps coming to  power and then implementing policies  that continue to only help the rich that  could be another source so whether this  is sustainable strategy I don't know  it's done some remarkable things in  parts of India and creating coalition's  we never thought we were gonna see in  Indian politics but how long those  coalition's could last is another story  okay so it's it's become a strategy to  implement you know these people to go  out into the field and then to your  point of them swaying the vote and then  implementing policies do you see is  there any research or have you seen  anything in terms of you know once the  party gets is elected and is in office  are they then going to try and implement  policy that will help the people  everyone who voted for t